text:
A system that only works is a demo. A system whose failure you can predict, trigger, and explain is engineering — and almost nobody does it.
Options, all rehearsable:

disconnect a sensor mid-run;
hold a breakpoint until ORE sets, then show recovery or its absence;
send an out-of-range command and show it refused;
stall an actuator and show the current draw or the reset.

State the prediction before you trigger it. Predicting correctly is worth more than the failure being graceful.

Division of labour is expected. Not knowing where your own code’s signals go is not. Be able to say:

which pins your code touches, and where they go physically;
what your part costs in the timing budget;
which requirement your part is responsible for.

Laboratory mission

Bring the whole system to the bench and prove it holds together: a resource map with no conflicts, a timing budget that fits, one fault found by bisection rather than guesswork, and a requirement list with a test beside each line.

You will show:

the resource map, complete and conflict-free;
the timing budget, summing to a stated percentage;
a 10-minute continuous run with telemetry and no resets;
one fault diagnosed by bisection, with the steps recorded;
requirements, tests, results, and an honest known-issues list.

HAL I2C, and finding what is there
/* Is anybody home? Sweep every address and report the ACKs. */
for (uint8_t a = 1; a &lt; 128; a++) {
    if (HAL_I2C_IsDeviceReady(&hi2c1, (uint16_t)(a &lt;&lt; 1), 2, 5) == HAL_OK) {
        /* report the 7-bit address 'a' over UART - L10's stream, reused */
    }
}

HAL_I2C_Master_Transmit(&hi2c1, 0x4E, data, len, 100);
HAL_I2C_Mem_Read(&hi2c1, 0xD0, reg, I2C_MEMADD_SIZE_8BIT, buf, n, 100);

A bus scan is the first thing to run and the last thing students think of. It answers “is it wired, powered, and at the address I believe?” in one step.

Loopback: proving the shift register
Connect MOSI to MISO with one jumper. The bus now talks to itself.
uint8_t tx = 0xA5, rx = 0;
HAL_SPI_TransmitReceive(&hspi2, &tx, &rx, 1, 100);
/* rx == 0xA5  -&gt;  clock, pins and shift register all work */

The cheapest diagnostic in the course: it separates “my SPI is misconfigured” from “the slave is not answering,” and needs no slave at all.

Laboratory mission

Put a real device on a real bus. Scan I2C to discover the LCD’s address, drive it to show live ADC readings, and prove an SPI link works with a single loopback jumper — then read both buses’ registers as evidence.

You will show:

a bus scan that finds the LCD and reports its 7-bit address;
the display showing live millivolts, refreshed at a justified rate;
AF set deliberately by addressing a device that is not there;
SPI loopback returning the byte you sent, with CPOL/CPHA read from CR1;
one individual variation, predicted before flashing.

The generated init
huart2.Instance          = USART2;
huart2.Init.BaudRate     = 115200;
huart2.Init.WordLength   = UART_WORDLENGTH_8B;
huart2.Init.StopBits     = UART_STOPBITS_1;
huart2.Init.Parity       = UART_PARITY_NONE;
huart2.Init.Mode         = UART_MODE_TX_RX;   /* both directions */
huart2.Init.OverSampling = UART_OVERSAMPLING_16;
HAL_UART_Init(&huart2);
HAL_UART_Init computes and writes BRR. Everything above is a request; BRR is what the hardware actually does.

Building a line
char    buf[48];
uint16_t raw = adc_read(ADC_CHANNEL_0);
uint32_t mv  = (raw * 3300u) / 4095u;

int n = snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), "raw=%u mv=%lu\r\n",
                 (unsigned)raw, (unsigned long)mv);

HAL_UART_Transmit(&huart2, (uint8_t *)buf, (uint16_t)n, 100);


01 snprintf cannot overflow buf — it truncates instead. sprintf can and will.


02 It returns the length it wanted; clamp before using it if truncation is possible.


03 \r\n — most serial monitors expect both.

Telemetry from the tick
if ((g_ms - t_tx) &gt;= 100U) {          /* 10 Hz, scheduled by L07 */
    t_tx = g_ms;
    uint16_t raw = adc_read(ADC_CHANNEL_0);
    int n = snprintf(buf, sizeof buf, "%lu,%u\r\n",
                     (unsigned long)g_ms, (unsigned)raw);
    HAL_UART_Transmit(&huart2, (uint8_t *)buf, (uint16_t)n, 100);
}

Comma-separated with a timestamp: paste it straight into a spreadsheet and plot it. Telemetry you cannot plot is just scrolling.

Receive by interrupt
uint8_t rx_byte;

/* arm it once, after HAL_UART_Init */
HAL_UART_Receive_IT(&huart2, &rx_byte, 1);

void HAL_UART_RxCpltCallback(UART_HandleTypeDef *huart)
{
    if (huart-&gt;Instance == USART2) {
        rx_ring[head] = rx_byte;          /* record - nothing else */
        head = (head + 1) % RX_SIZE;
        HAL_UART_Receive_IT(&huart2, &rx_byte, 1);   /* re-arm! */
    }
}

Re-arming is mandatory. HAL disarms after each completed receive. Forget the re-arm and you get exactly one byte, ever — then silence that looks like broken hardware.

Assembling a command
/* in main(): drain what the callback recorded */
while (tail != head) {
    char c = rx_ring[tail];
    tail = (tail + 1) % RX_SIZE;

    if (c == '\r' || c == '\n') {
        line[len] = '\0';
        handle_command(line);           /* act in main, not in the ISR */
        len = 0;
    } else if (len &lt; sizeof(line) - 1) {
        line[len++] = c;                /* bounds check: always */
    }
}
The callback records bytes; main() decides what they mean. Same discipline as every interrupt since L06.

A tiny command set
void handle_command(const char *s)
{
    if      (strcmp(s, "led on")  == 0) { led_mode = 1; }
    else if (strcmp(s, "led off") == 0) { led_mode = 0; }
    else if (strncmp(s, "duty ", 5) == 0) {
        int d = atoi(s + 5);
        if (d &gt;= 0 && d &lt;= 999) { __HAL_TIM_SET_COMPARE(&htim2, TIM_CHANNEL_1, d); }
        else { reply("range 0..999\r\n"); }
    }
    else { reply("? unknown\r\n"); }
}

Always reply — including to a bad command. A silent device is indistinguishable from a dead one.

Making printf work
int __io_putchar(int ch)                 /* newlib calls this per character */
{
    HAL_UART_Transmit(&huart2, (uint8_t *)&ch, 1, 10);
    return ch;
}


01 Convenient — and expensive: printf with floats pulls in a large library and a lot of stack.


02 One HAL_UART_Transmit per character is far slower than one per line.


03 Fine for a banner at startup; wrong for a 10 Hz telemetry stream.

Laboratory mission

Make the board talk and listen. Stream timestamped ADC readings at 10 Hz over the ST-LINK virtual COM port, then accept typed commands that change the LED duty — with the tick, the PWM, and the ADC all still running underneath.

You will show:

BRR matching your own hand-computed value;
telemetry at a measured 10 Hz, non-blocking, pasteable into a spreadsheet;
interrupt-driven receive with a correct re-arm;
a command that acts, and a bad command that is refused politely;
a deliberate ORE, observed in SR and explained.

char buf[48];
int n = snprintf(buf, sizeof buf, "%lu,%u\r\n",
                 (unsigned long)g_ms, (unsigned)raw);
HAL_UART_Transmit(&huart2, (uint8_t *)buf, (uint16_t)n, 100);

snprintf, never sprintf. snprintf truncates at the buffer size; sprintf writes past the end and corrupts whatever is next on the stack.
HAL_UART_Transmit blocks until the last byte is handed over — 1.56 ms for an 18-character line at 115 200. Acceptable in main() at 10 Hz; never acceptable in a callback.
\r\n, not \n alone — most monitors need both to return to column 1.
Comma-separated with a timestamp pastes straight into a spreadsheet.

TXE versus TC. TXE means “the holding register is free, give me the next byte” — the previous byte may still be shifting out. TC means the shift register is empty too and the line is idle. Powering down, or turning a half-duplex transceiver around, needs TC.

HAL_UART_Receive_IT(&huart2, &rx_byte, 1);        /* arm once */

void HAL_UART_RxCpltCallback(UART_HandleTypeDef *huart)
{
    if (huart-&gt;Instance == USART2) {
        rx_ring[head] = rx_byte;
        head = (head + 1) % RX_SIZE;
        HAL_UART_Receive_IT(&huart2, &rx_byte, 1);  /* RE-ARM - mandatory */
    }
}

Never HAL_UART_Receive blocking. It waits for a human; meanwhile nothing else in the system runs.
The re-arm is mandatory. HAL disarms after each completed receive. Omit it and you receive exactly one byte, ever — a silence that looks like broken hardware.
The callback records; main() decides. Same discipline as every interrupt since L06.
Reading DR clears RXNE — read-to-clear, like the ADC’s EOC.

ORE. Set when a byte arrives before the previous one was read. That byte is gone, and HAL delivers nothing further until the error is cleared. Usual causes: blocking somewhere long, a missing re-arm, or a breakpoint held while data arrives.

The generated init
hadc1.Instance                = ADC1;
hadc1.Init.ClockPrescaler     = ADC_CLOCK_SYNC_PCLK_DIV2;  // 16 MHz -&gt; 8 MHz
hadc1.Init.Resolution         = ADC_RESOLUTION_12B;        // 0..4095
hadc1.Init.ContinuousConvMode = DISABLE;                   // one shot per start
hadc1.Init.EOCSelection       = ADC_EOC_SINGLE_CONV;       // flag per conversion
hadc1.Init.DataAlign          = ADC_DATAALIGN_RIGHT;
hadc1.Init.NbrOfConversion    = 1;
HAL_ADC_Init(&hadc1);

ADC_ChannelConfTypeDef sConfig = {0};
sConfig.Channel      = ADC_CHANNEL_0;                      // PA0 = IN0 = A0
sConfig.Rank         = 1;
sConfig.SamplingTime = ADC_SAMPLETIME_84CYCLES;            // ~12 us total
HAL_ADC_ConfigChannel(&hadc1, &sConfig);
Resolution writes RES in CR1; SamplingTime writes SMPR2; the channel lands in SQR3.

Deep dive: UM1725 Rev 8, ADC generic driver — p. 56.

The interrupt option
HAL_ADC_Start_IT(&hadc1);             // one conversion, then interrupt

void HAL_ADC_ConvCpltCallback(ADC_HandleTypeDef *hadc)
{
    if (hadc-&gt;Instance == ADC1) {
        g_raw = (uint16_t)HAL_ADC_GetValue(hadc);   // integer only here
    }
}


01 The L06 path, one more source: enable ADC global interrupt in NVIC first.


02 Start one conversion per tick; the callback stores, the main loop consumes.


03 Continuous + interrupt would fire every 12 µs—83 000 interrupts per second. Pace it.



Deep dive: UM1725 Rev 8, HAL_ADC_Start_IT — p. 66, HAL_ADC_ConvCpltCallback — p. 68.

Laboratory mission

Read the knob, command the servo. Read the light, dim LD2. One ADC, two sensors—driving the actuators you built in L08.

You will show:

the ADC configuration justified: 12-bit, 84-cycle sampling, single conversion;
raw and millivolts live in the debugger, matching your hand calculation;
the knob commanding the servo through the 1000–2000 µs contract;
a night-light: darkness raises LD2’s brightness through the divider math;
one individual variation, predicted before flashing.

/* CubeMX: PA0 -&gt; ADC1_IN0, PA1 -&gt; ADC1_IN1, 12-bit,
   continuous DISABLED, EOC single conversion, 84-cycle sampling. */

uint16_t adc_read(uint32_t ch)
{
    ADC_ChannelConfTypeDef s = {0};
    s.Channel      = ch;
    s.Rank         = 1;
    s.SamplingTime = ADC_SAMPLETIME_84CYCLES;
    HAL_ADC_ConfigChannel(&hadc1, &s);

    HAL_ADC_Start(&hadc1);
    HAL_ADC_PollForConversion(&hadc1, 10);   /* waits for EOC */
    uint16_t raw = HAL_ADC_GetValue(&hadc1); /* reads DR, clears EOC */
    HAL_ADC_Stop(&hadc1);
    return raw;
}
Pace the reads from the tick, never from HAL_Delay:
if ((g_ms - t_adc) &gt;= 10U) {            /* L07 tick schedules the read */
    t_adc = g_ms;
    uint16_t raw  = adc_read(ADC_CHANNEL_0);
    uint32_t duty = (raw * 999u) / 4095u;             /* L08's ARR */
    __HAL_TIM_SET_COMPARE(&htim2, TIM_CHANNEL_1, duty);
}
Sampling time. The input must charge the sample-and-hold capacitor through the source’s own resistance. A low-impedance source (a pot wiper) charges fast; a high-impedance one (an LDR divider, a thermistor) needs longer. Too short and the reading is dragged toward the previous channel’s value — the classic symptom of cross-talk when switching channels.
One ADC, many channels. There is one converter behind a multiplexer. To read two channels you reconfigure rank 1 and convert again — which is what adc_read(ch) does. Both readings are therefore taken at slightly different moments, not simultaneously.

The generated init
htim2.Instance               = TIM2;
htim2.Init.Prescaler         = 15;     // 16 MHz -&gt; 1 MHz: 1 tick = 1 us
htim2.Init.CounterMode       = TIM_COUNTERMODE_UP;
htim2.Init.Period            = 999;    // 1 MHz -&gt; 1 kHz PWM
htim2.Init.AutoReloadPreload = TIM_AUTORELOAD_PRELOAD_ENABLE;
HAL_TIM_PWM_Init(&htim2);

TIM_OC_InitTypeDef sConfigOC = {0};
sConfigOC.OCMode     = TIM_OCMODE_PWM1;      // high while CNT &lt; CCR
sConfigOC.Pulse      = 0;                    // initial CCR1: 0 % duty
sConfigOC.OCPolarity = TIM_OCPOLARITY_HIGH;
HAL_TIM_PWM_ConfigChannel(&htim2, &sConfigOC, TIM_CHANNEL_1);
Pulse is the HAL’s name for the CCR value—just as Period was its name for ARR.

Deep dive: UM1725 Rev 8, TIM functions — p. 1065.

A breathing LED
HAL_TIM_PWM_Start(&htim2, TIM_CHANNEL_1);

uint32_t t_fade = 0; int32_t duty = 0, dir = +5;
while (1) {
    if ((g_ms - t_fade) &gt;= 10U) {          // L07's tick pattern
        t_fade = g_ms;
        duty += dir;
        if (duty &gt;= 1000 || duty &lt;= 0) dir = -dir;
        __HAL_TIM_SET_COMPARE(&htim2, TIM_CHANNEL_1, (uint32_t)duty);
    }
    /* other work continues */
}

The timer makes the light; the 1 ms tick schedules the changes. Two timers, two jobs, zero blocking.

The capture callback
void HAL_TIM_IC_CaptureCallback(TIM_HandleTypeDef *htim)
{
    if (htim-&gt;Instance == TIM4 &&
        htim-&gt;Channel == HAL_TIM_ACTIVE_CHANNEL_1) {
        uint16_t now = HAL_TIM_ReadCapturedValue(htim, TIM_CHANNEL_1);
        period_us = (uint16_t)(now - last);   // wrap-safe on 16 bits
        last = now;
    }
}


01 One shared callback for all capture channels—check instance and channel.


02 ReadCapturedValue returns CCR1—the hardware’s timestamp.


03 The cast keeps the subtraction 16-bit—wrap-safe, as since Week 6.



Deep dive: UM1725 Rev 8, HAL_TIM_ReadCapturedValue — p. 1097.

Laboratory mission

Close the loop. TIM2 generates PWM on PA5, one jumper carries it to PB6, and TIM4 measures it back—frequency and duty compared against the values you configured.

You will show:

PSC/ARR/CCR chosen from 16 MHz, with units explained;
the AF handover of PA5 in the .ioc and the generated code;
a live duty change from the SFR view;
measured period and duty matching the generating side;
a live polarity flip, and one intentional overcapture via CC1OF;
one individual variation, predicted before flashing.

/* rising edges, channel 1 direct */
uint16_t now = HAL_TIM_ReadCapturedValue(htim, TIM_CHANNEL_1);
period_us = (uint16_t)(now - last);     /* cast: wrap-safe on 16 bits */
last = now;

/* falling edge, channel 2 indirect - high time since the last rising edge */
high_us = (uint16_t)(HAL_TIM_ReadCapturedValue(htim, TIM_CHANNEL_2) - last);
\[f_{\text{signal}} = \frac{1}{\text{period}} \qquad
\text{duty} = \frac{\text{high\_us}}{\text{period\_us}}\]

The cast is not decoration. On a 16-bit timer the subtraction must be done in 16 bits: captures 64000 then 1500 give (uint16_t)(1500 - 64000) = 3036, the true elapsed count. Without the cast you get a large negative number.
Direct vs indirect. CH1 direct listens to its own pin (TI1); CH2 indirect listens to TI1 as well, so both channels watch one signal on opposite edges. CH2 configured direct would listen to PB7 instead — and capture nothing.
Range. A 16-bit timer at a 1 µs tick measures honestly up to 65 536 µs ≈ 65.5 ms, i.e. signals down to about 15.3 Hz. Slower than that, the counter wraps more than once between edges and the number lies silently.

L06 recap: the blocking delay
The L04 blink—and every version since—wastes the CPU while it waits:
while (1) {
    HAL_GPIO_TogglePin(GPIOA, GPIO_PIN_5);
    HAL_Delay(500);           // CPU does nothing useful here
}


1 What is the CPU doing during the 500 ms?


2 Could the L06 button event be serviced during the delay? Could the main loop react to it?



The interrupt still fires and the callback still runs—but the main loop cannot respond until HAL_Delay returns.

Configuring the time base
TIM_HandleTypeDef htim2;

htim2.Instance           = TIM2;
htim2.Init.Prescaler     = 15999;              // 16 MHz -&gt; 1 kHz
htim2.Init.CounterMode   = TIM_COUNTERMODE_UP;
htim2.Init.Period        = 999;                // 1 kHz -&gt; 1 s
HAL_TIM_Base_Init(&htim2);


01 The handle structure collects the timer’s settings—GPIO_InitTypeDef’s pattern again.


02 Period is the HAL’s name for the ARR value.


03 In a CubeMX project this code is generated as MX_TIM2_Init().



Deep dive: UM1725 Rev 8, TIM base functions — p. 1065.

Polling the update flag
HAL_TIM_Base_Start(&htim2);

while (1) {
    /* other work runs freely here */

    if (__HAL_TIM_GET_FLAG(&htim2, TIM_FLAG_UPDATE) != RESET) {
        __HAL_TIM_CLEAR_FLAG(&htim2, TIM_FLAG_UPDATE);
        HAL_GPIO_TogglePin(GPIOA, GPIO_PIN_5);   // once per second
    }
}





Check the flag, act, clear the flag—an uncleared UIF reads as an instant new event.

Interrupt mode: setup and callback
HAL_NVIC_SetPriority(TIM2_IRQn, 0, 0);
HAL_NVIC_EnableIRQ(TIM2_IRQn);
HAL_TIM_Base_Start_IT(&htim2);     // start + enable update interrupt
void HAL_TIM_PeriodElapsedCallback(TIM_HandleTypeDef *htim)
{
    if (htim-&gt;Instance == TIM2) {
        HAL_GPIO_TogglePin(GPIOA, GPIO_PIN_5);
    }
}


01 Start_IT sets UIE in TIM2-&gt;DIER; the NVIC lines are L06’s checklist with a new request name.


02 HAL clears UIF, then calls the one shared callback—check htim-&gt;Instance.


03 CubeMX generates TIM2_IRQHandler() in stm32f4xx_it.c—verify it exists; do not write your own.

The 1 ms tick
The most useful timer configuration in embedded practice—one fast timer, one counter:
volatile uint32_t ms = 0;

/* PSC = 15  -&gt; 16 MHz / 16   = 1 MHz
   ARR = 999 -&gt; 1 MHz / 1000  = 1 kHz -&gt; update every 1 ms */

void HAL_TIM_PeriodElapsedCallback(TIM_HandleTypeDef *htim)
{
    if (htim-&gt;Instance == TIM2) {
        ms++;
    }
}
ms is the L06 button_event pattern generalized: the callback reports, main() consumes. volatile for the same reason.

Live demo idea: comment out volatile with optimization on and show the loop freeze — or just promise it for the lab.

Many intervals, one timer
uint32_t t_led = 0, t_sample = 0;

while (1) {
    if ((ms - t_led) &gt;= 500U) {          // every 500 ms
        t_led = ms;
        HAL_GPIO_TogglePin(GPIOA, GPIO_PIN_5);
    }
    if ((ms - t_sample) &gt;= 100U) {       // every 100 ms
        t_sample = ms;
        /* read a sensor */
    }
    /* other work continues */
}

Unsigned subtraction keeps every schedule correct when ms eventually wraps—the same arithmetic as L06’s debounce.

A free-running microsecond timer
Leave ARR at its 32-bit maximum and the counter itself becomes a clock:
/* PSC = 15 -&gt; 1 MHz: CNT increments once per microsecond */
uint32_t start = TIM2-&gt;CNT;

do_work();

uint32_t elapsed_us = TIM2-&gt;CNT - start;   // wrap-safe


01 No interrupts at all—just read the counter twice and subtract.


02 TIM2 and TIM5 are the F401’s 32-bit timers: at 1 MHz they wrap after ~71 minutes.


03 This is how you measure code instead of guessing.

HAL vs bare-metal


STM32 HAL
htim2.Instance       = TIM2;
htim2.Init.Prescaler = 15999;
htim2.Init.Period    = 999;
HAL_TIM_Base_Init(&htim2);
HAL_TIM_Base_Start(&htim2);

DIRECT CMSIS ACCESS
RCC-&gt;APB1ENR |= (1U &lt;&lt; 0);  // TIM2EN
TIM2-&gt;PSC = 15999;
TIM2-&gt;ARR = 999;
TIM2-&gt;CR1 |= (1U &lt;&lt; 0);     // CEN


L05’s conclusion holds: same registers, different interface. The clock-enable-first rule from L04 also holds—TIM2 is on APB1, not AHB1.

Laboratory mission

Blink LD2 every 500 ms from a TIM2 update interrupt—no HAL_Delay() anywhere—then build the 1 ms tick and schedule two independent intervals.

You will show:

PSC/ARR calculated from 16 MHz, with the +1s explained;
a breakpoint proving HAL_TIM_PeriodElapsedCallback runs;
TIM2-&gt;CNT moving and UIF setting in the SFR view;
two software intervals driven by one ms counter;
one individual period change, predicted before flashing.


Checkoff pacing: A and B before the break; C and the extension after. The SFR proof is the non-negotiable part.

/* Polling: two operations, both required */
if (TIM2-&gt;SR & TIM_SR_UIF) {    /* 1. test the flag  */
    TIM2-&gt;SR &= ~TIM_SR_UIF;    /* 2. clear it       */
    /* ... one period has elapsed ... */
}

/* Interrupt: HAL clears UIF for you before calling this */
volatile uint32_t g_ms = 0;

void HAL_TIM_PeriodElapsedCallback(TIM_HandleTypeDef *htim)
{
    if (htim-&gt;Instance == TIM2) {   /* one callback serves every timer */
        g_ms++;                     /* record - nothing else          */
    }
}
Start it with HAL_TIM_Base_Start_IT(&htim2); — Start alone runs the counter but never enables UIE, so the callback stays silent.

Test and clear. A polling loop that forgets the clear sees the flag still set on the next pass and fires continuously.
Callback rules, unchanged from L06: check the source, record, return fast. A callback that takes 2 ms cannot keep up with a 1 ms tick.
volatile: the interrupt changes g_ms between two main-loop instructions.

uint32_t t_phase = 0;
uint8_t  led_on  = 0;

while (1) {
    uint32_t wait = led_on ? 100U : 900U;
    if ((g_ms - t_phase) &gt;= wait) {   /* unsigned: wrap-safe */
        t_phase = g_ms;
        led_on = !led_on;
        HAL_GPIO_WritePin(GPIOA, GPIO_PIN_5,
                          led_on ? GPIO_PIN_SET : GPIO_PIN_RESET);
    }
}
The unsigned subtraction g_ms - t_phase stays correct across wrap-around — the same arithmetic that made L06’s debounce safe.
Drift. t_phase = g_ms re-anchors to the moment the toggle actually happened, so every late pass pushes all later toggles: error accumulates. t_phase += 500U keeps the deadlines anchored to the original schedule: one toggle is late, the rest are not. Choose deliberately.

From polling to interrupts
while (1) {
    GPIO_PinState button =
        HAL_GPIO_ReadPin(GPIOC, GPIO_PIN_13);

    if (button == GPIO_PIN_RESET) {
        HAL_GPIO_WritePin(GPIOA, GPIO_PIN_5,
                          GPIO_PIN_SET);
    } else {
        HAL_GPIO_WritePin(GPIOA, GPIO_PIN_5,
                          GPIO_PIN_RESET);
    }
}
This is the L05 program. The loop checks PC13 repeatedly—even when the button has not changed.

Polling asks, “Did it happen yet?” again and again.

L05 exit ticket - the answers
config.Mode = GPIO_MODE_INPUT;
config.Pull = GPIO_NOPULL;
HAL_GPIO_Init(GPIOC, &config);


01 REGISTER — PC13’s MODER bits 27:26 = 00 and PUPDR bits 27:26 = 00.


02 POINTER — the function reads your members through the address; no copy is made.


03 LOGIC — GPIO_PIN_RESET: pressing grounds PC13; the external pull-up holds it high only while released.


04 EVIDENCE — the SFR view: both PC13 fields read 00 (and IDR bit 13 follows the physical button).


And today, that polling loop above stops asking — the button will tell us.

The IRQ handler

PRESS→ NOTICE→ ASK→ LOOK UP→ RUN YOUR CODE handler

void EXTI15_10_IRQHandler(void)
{
    HAL_GPIO_EXTI_IRQHandler(GPIO_PIN_13);
}
The interrupt handler is connected to the vector table.
The processor enters this function automatically when it accepts the request.
We do not call EXTI15_10_IRQHandler() from main().

The HAL callback

PRESS→ NOTICE→ ASK→ LOOK UP→ RUN YOUR CODE callback

void HAL_GPIO_EXTI_Callback(uint16_t GPIO_Pin)
{
    if (GPIO_Pin == GPIO_PIN_13) {
        button_event = 1;
    }
}
We write the callback, but HAL decides when to call it.
The parameter tells us which GPIO pin produced the event.
uint16_t is the fixed-width type from &lt;stdint.h&gt; used in the C weeks: exactly 16 unsigned bits—one bit per pin of a GPIO port.

Generated initialization code
The important generated lines are:
GPIO_InitStruct.Pin  = GPIO_PIN_13;
GPIO_InitStruct.Mode = GPIO_MODE_IT_FALLING;
GPIO_InitStruct.Pull = GPIO_NOPULL;
HAL_GPIO_Init(GPIOC, &GPIO_InitStruct);

HAL_NVIC_SetPriority(EXTI15_10_IRQn, 0, 0);
HAL_NVIC_EnableIRQ(EXTI15_10_IRQn);
HAL_GPIO_Init writes the registers we met: the SYSCFG routing field, EXTI-&gt;IMR, and EXTI-&gt;FTSR.
HAL_NVIC_EnableIRQ allows the request to reach the processor.
These are the names CubeMX generates—you will find these exact lines in your project.

First test: toggle LD2
void HAL_GPIO_EXTI_Callback(uint16_t GPIO_Pin)
{
    if (GPIO_Pin == GPIO_PIN_13) {
        HAL_GPIO_TogglePin(GPIOA, GPIO_PIN_5);
    }
}
If one press changes LD2, the complete interrupt path works.
This is a test checkpoint, not yet the best application structure.

Recording the event
void HAL_GPIO_EXTI_Callback(uint16_t GPIO_Pin)
{
    if (GPIO_Pin == GPIO_PIN_13) {
        button_event = 1;
    }
}
The callback does not toggle the LED and does not wait.
It only reports: “B1 was pressed.”

Consuming the event
